\subsubsection{Clinical Utility}
On the independent hold-out clinical set (n=10,383; 1,276 AS, 9,107 HC), the ensemble achieved 99.5\% sensitivity (95\% CI 99.0--100.0) and 86.8\% specificity (95\% CI 86.2--87.4) at an optimal threshold of 0.62 (Youden's index). Precision was 82.9\%. Robust generalization was evidenced by consistent AUROC across subgroups (age <50 vs. $\geq$50 years, gender, disease severity; ΔAUROC<0.01). This calibration enhances clinical decision support by providing reliable probability estimates, reducing false negatives in AS diagnosis.

